// SSU instructional materials 2025-2026: evidence summaries, goals 6, 7, 8 and 9.
// These render publicly under Evidence Summary in the campus report.

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.2-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State has no documented process requiring accessibility verification when course materials are selected, authored or procured. No office holds responsibility for such a check. No record shows an accessibility verification performed at the point a course material was chosen or commissioned. The campus is recorded as Not Started because the indicator's subject has no process, no assigned owner and no output. Accessibility supports that act later in the course lifecycle are recorded under the indicators that cover them.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.4-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State reviews online courses for accessibility before posting through a quality certification path that faculty enter voluntarily. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology runs the Quality Learning and Teaching certification, where a course must reach 85 percent or better conformance before it goes to the review committee. CTET publishes accessibility guidance covering documents, multimedia and course design. UDOIT identifies accessibility problems inside Canvas courses, and CTET trains faculty to correct them before publishing. The campus reaches Defined because a review path exists with a stated conformance threshold. It does not reach Established because the certification path is voluntary, so no requirement reaches courses outside it. No position description assigns responsibility for review before posting. No record shows how many courses were reviewed or remediated before release.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.5-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State incorporates accessibility into course redesign through its quality certification path. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology runs that path in three levels with fixed cohort dates. Accessibility is taught inside the first level. The Quality Learning and Teaching rubric applies accessibility criteria at the third level, where a course must reach 85 percent or better conformance before the review committee sees it. Sonoma pays a stipend at each level and covers the external review fee. The campus reaches Defined because redesign work meets accessibility criteria at a stated threshold. It does not reach Established because no owner is recorded for the certification path. No record compares a course before and after redesign, which is the output the indicator asks for.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.7-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State evaluates Canvas courses for accessibility on a recurring schedule, using an automated tool and manual review. UDOIT scans Canvas courses across the campus. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology ranks courses by UDOIT score together with enrolment, and works the ranked list each term. Student workers record a course's UDOIT score before remediation and run the scan again when the work closes. Seventy-two courses were worked in summer 2026. Twenty-one courses had reached 98 percent or better by October 2026. Tim Hensel holds the Accessibility Specialist role, and the Canvas remediation programme is named in his position description. The campus reaches Defined because the evaluation runs on a term schedule with defined methods and an assigned owner. It does not reach Established because no evaluation record leaves the tool. A plan to produce an annual accessibility snapshot for reporting is in progress.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.8-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State prioritises and remediates inaccessible Canvas course content through a standing programme with a named owner. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology ranks courses by UDOIT score and enrolment, taking fully online asynchronous courses first. Each course opens one service ticket with a standardised subject line, so the work can be reported on afterwards. Standard messages go to the instructor at each step. Deans approve each term's course list, and instructors may decline, which about one instructor does per semester. Remediation happens inside the live Canvas course without altering the instructor's content. Twenty-one courses had reached 98 percent or better by October 2026. Tim Hensel holds the Accessibility Specialist role, and both the student remediation team and the Canvas remediation programme are named in his position description. The campus reaches Established because the work follows a stated ranking through a ticketed workflow, and responsibility is documented in a position description. It does not reach Managed because no measure of success is defined and no progress record is exported from the ticket system. The remediation team dropped from seven staff to four during the year.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-6.9-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State produces alternative access plans for products that do not meet accessibility standards, using the systemwide template. Two signed plans are on file, for Anatomy LT and for Insights Discovery Profiles. Sean Place owns the Anatomy LT plan and Elizabeth Stanny owns the Insights Discovery Profiles plan. The SFBRN IT Accessibility Review Procedure sets out when a plan is required, how it is drafted, how it is routed for signature and where it is filed. That procedure rates a product's risk and requires a signed plan before a high-risk product is approved. The campus reaches Defined because completed plans exist on a standard template and a written procedure governs how they are made. It does not reach Established because the regional procurement process operates that procedure rather than Sonoma. No Sonoma process states when a plan is required for the learning management system or its integrations specifically. Neither plan records a review date.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-7.1-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State documents faculty responsibility for accessible instructional materials in Academic Senate policy. The Syllabus policy states that syllabi shall be provided in a format accessible to all students with the content built into the university learning management system. That requirement is mandatory and applies to every course. The Disability Access for Students policy states that faculty should work with appropriate campus personnel to ensure that electronic and all other instructional information for their courses is in an accessible and suitable format. The same policy requires faculty to respond promptly to approved accommodation requests, and to review alternative formats with the student's adviser in advance of need. The Course Instructional Materials Adoption policy places selection of instructional materials with the faculty member of record. The campus reaches Established because faculty responsibility is set out in published policy recommended by the Academic Senate. It does not reach Managed because no measure records faculty compliance with either policy. The broader duty covering all instructional information is written as a recommendation rather than a requirement. The Disability Access policy's scheduled review date passed in July 2016.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-7.3-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State distributes accessible templates to faculty and does not produce examples of remediated instructional materials. The accessible syllabus template is published on the campus accessibility site. The SSU Canvas Course Design Template is available to faculty through Canvas Commons. CTET's published guidance directs faculty toward accessible formats, including using a Canvas page in place of a PDF. John Lynch stated that Sonoma does not produce comparative examples showing a document before and after remediation. A past exercise rebuilding a PDF with faculty was not repeated. The campus reaches Defined because templates are published and reach faculty. It does not reach Established because the indicator asks for examples of accessible instructional materials, and a template is a starting point rather than an example. Tim Hensel proposed saving a real document alongside its remediated version, and that work has not started.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-7.5-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State provides faculty with tools, workshops and a remediation service for creating accessible instructional content. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology runs accessibility workshops in person and online each semester. An ATI faculty responsibilities video is distributed to all faculty at the start of each year. The accessible syllabus template gives faculty an accessible starting document. Disability Services for Students licenses EquatIO for mathematical content from an outside supplier. Disability Services also converts course materials into accessible formats on request. The campus reaches Defined because the resources exist and faculty are told about them at the start of each year. It does not reach Established because the workshop series and the faculty responsibilities video have no documentation recording what they contain or who attended. An open recommendation states that faculty are not told Adobe Acrobat is available to them through the campus Adobe licence.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-7.11-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State applies accessibility checks when the library acquires electronic resources and when it digitises items from its collection. Vendors must supply an accessibility conformance report or an accessibility statement when the library acquires a new database. Library staff review the evaluation method and the conformance level stated in that documentation. Items requested from the physical collection are scanned and delivered as accessible PDFs. Students with accommodations request conversion of print library materials through Disability Services for Students. Assistive technology is available in the library and through Disability Services. The campus reaches Defined because accessibility is checked at acquisition and at digitisation, and library staff describe the practice consistently. It does not reach Established because the library states no threshold for acceptable conformance, so a product supported only in part can be acquired without a recorded decision. No written workflow covers accessibility when library assets are created or maintained. No owner is recorded for this work.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-8.1-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State communicates accessibility responsibilities to faculty each year without a written communication plan behind it. An ATI faculty responsibilities video goes to all faculty at the start of each year and is reinforced at new faculty orientation. Sandra Ayala presents a poster setting out six areas committee members promote in their own departments: timely book orders, accessible syllabi, document formatting, accessible PDFs, alternative text for images, and video captioning. CTET publishes accessibility guidance and runs standalone workshops. Physical signage and banners appear on campus. CTET allocates a quarter of its instructional design staff responsibilities to this work. The campus reaches Defined because recurring communication reaches all faculty through named channels. It does not reach Established because no written communication plan exists, and a plan to publish one is in progress. A record for 2024-2025 states that the formal campaign did not run as planned because of staffing and leadership turnover. The printed ATI piece now circulates as a photographed image rather than as text, so a screen reader cannot read it.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-8.3-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State provides accessibility training materials in several formats and for several audiences. The ATI faculty responsibilities video reaches all faculty annually. CTET runs accessibility workshops in person and online, and offers Canvas professional development at self-paced and facilitated levels. The Canvas Remediation Manual is the written training path for the student remediation team. Student workers complete checklist-based training against defined competencies by set dates. The Accessible Syllabus Policy sets a required standard, and the accessible syllabus template gives faculty a document to start from. The Instructional Materials Subcommittee sets annual priorities and records them in a plan submitted each year. Training resources from the California Community Colleges were added to the regional accessibility resources page in September 2026. The campus reaches Established because training exists across content and delivery modes, and reaches both faculty and student staff. It does not reach Managed because no record shows who completed which training. Five of the seven items listed here have no documentation attached, and the remediation manual sits inside a Canvas course that cannot be opened from outside CTET.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-8.11-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State includes accessibility in new faculty orientation, delivered by named staff. John Lynch runs the Canvas orientation segment, which includes a fifteen minute accessibility discussion. Disability Services for Students delivers a separate full hour on compliance obligations. The textbook adoption deadline is covered in the same orientation. John Lynch confirmed that he is the officially responsible person for the segment. The 2026 orientation agenda is on file. The campus reaches Defined because accessibility holds a fixed place in orientation with a named person delivering it. It does not reach Established because no attendance record exists for any cycle. No position description assigns the orientation segment to anyone.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-8.12-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State teaches accessibility inside its faculty development programme and pays faculty to complete it. The Center for Teaching and Educational Technology runs a Canvas professional development ladder in three published levels with fixed cohort dates. Accessibility is taught in the first level. The Quality Learning and Teaching rubric applies accessibility criteria at the third level. Sonoma pays stipends of 300 dollars at the first level, 300 at the second and 400 at the third, and pays a 750 dollar external review fee for Quality Learning and Teaching review. CTET ran a paid one day accessibility session in March 2026 with a 300 dollar stipend, led by John Lynch and Sandra Ayala. CTET offers roughly twelve accessibility workshop sessions a semester. The campus reaches Established because the programme is published with fixed dates, and the campus funds faculty participation. It does not reach Managed because participation is tracked internally with no record on file. No owner is recorded for the professional development ladder or for the Quality Learning and Teaching certification.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-8.13-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State reviews academic technology for accessibility through committee and purchase review, and holds no documented procedure of its own. The Academic Technology and Instructional Space Subcommittee considers major educational technology for the campus. Instructional technology purchases route through the SFBRN IT Accessibility Review Procedure, supported by an accessibility analyst and Procurement. The subcommittee's published page lists its membership and meeting schedule. That page sets out who serves rather than how a review is conducted. The campus reaches Defined because academic technology reaches an accessibility review, and the committee that considers it is constituted and published. It does not reach Established because no procedure records how the subcommittee reviews accessibility. Whether the subcommittee reviews instructional technology or only administrative systems is an open question.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-8.14-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State trains the student staff who remediate course content, and provides no equivalent programme for employees. Student workers on the remediation team complete checklist-based training against defined competencies by set dates. The Canvas Remediation Manual is the written training path they follow. Tim Hensel enrols students in external accessibility coursework when it is available, including courses taken in summer 2026. Accessible instructional materials responsibilities were added to Tim Hensel's position description in September 2026, which assigns the work formally. The Canvas professional development ladder is open to faculty and staff who choose to take it. The campus reaches Defined because training for the remediation team runs on a defined schedule against stated competencies, and responsibility for it is documented. It does not reach Established because no record shows which employees received which training. A concern on file states that staff outside the student team have no formal ongoing training path, with funding decided case by case.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-9.2-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State's ATI steering committee meets and sets annual instructional materials priorities, and no executive body approves the resulting plan. Sandra Ayala chairs the committee, which met to open the autumn 2026 semester. The committee covers instructional materials, procurement and web accessibility. The instructional materials group produces a prioritised list of indicators each autumn. The campus publishes an ATI programme overview describing the committee and its ambassadors. The campus reaches Defined because the committee is constituted, meets on a recurring basis and produces an annual priority list. It does not reach Established because the approval step the indicator names has no body to perform it. A concern on file states that neither Sonoma nor the regional network has an executive committee able to receive and approve the annual plan. The 2024-2025 plan was never approved.";

MATCH (yse:YearSuccessEvidence {year_identifier: '2025-2026-9.3-ins-ssu'})
SET yse.admin_review_description = "Sonoma State's instructional materials subcommittee was reconstituted in September 2026 and now meets several times a semester. John Lynch handed day-to-day organisation of the group to Sandra Ayala and Tim Hensel, both of whom accepted. Sandra Ayala acts as Faculty Fellow and is paid for that responsibility through the teaching centre budget. Tim Hensel acts in the Accessibility Specialist role added to his position description in September 2026. Disability Services for Students and the bookstore are standing participants. The subcommittee sets annual priorities and records them in a plan submitted each year. The campus reaches Defined because the group meets on a fixed cadence with named organisers and funded roles. It does not reach Established because no minutes, charter or membership record is on file for the subcommittee. The annual instructional materials plan created in spring 2026 was sent onward and not acted upon.";
